{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1789805339022,"sessionID":"ses_f474d3efbffe9lZiMOJ7zaElyO","part":{"id":"prt_0b8b6218b001d2vhJhN9dR7c4j","messageID":"msg_0b8b610b30016iCaIlzePwm306","sessionID":"ses_f474d3efbffe9lZiMOJ7zaElyO","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1789805372177,"sessionID":"ses_f474d3efbffe9lZiMOJ7zaElyO","part":{"id":"prt_0b8b6218d001PkF43p157iaVJx","messageID":"msg_0b8b610b30016iCaIlzePwm306","sessionID":"ses_f474d3efbffe9lZiMOJ7zaElyO","type":"text","text":"Now I need to review the edge cases carefully:\n\n1. **Write-fail branch**: `Prior::Created` for a file that did not exist → `rollback` will attempt `remove_file` → fails (ENoent) → `failed` gets the path → `RollbackIncomplete` incorrectly reported even though the tree is clean (no file to delete, nothing was written). Wait — actually, if the write failed and the file did not exist, `remove_file` fails with ENOENT. That's a spurious \"failure.\" Is that acceptable? The spec says \"A rollback that completes reports WriteFailed.\" If the rollback \"completes\" but the delete of a never-created file fails… hmm, the spec says \"restore every file written by this call to its prior content (or delete it if this call created it)\". The file was not written (write failed), so arguably it should not be in the rollback list at all. But if a parent directory was created by `create_dir_all`, we need to clean that up. The prior list should contain only files that were actually written.\n\nLet me rethink: in the write-fail branch, the file was not written. So the correct rollback list is just `prior` (the files that were actually written). Parent cleanup still happens because `rollback` cleans up the parents of all entries (and also the failed path's parent, but… that is not in the list). Hmm, the failed path's parent may have been created by `create_dir_all` in this call. If we don't clean it up, an empty directory remains. But the spec says \"the target tree must contain no backup, temp, or stray files created by this call\" — directories may count as \"stray\". To be safe, the cleanup should also include the failed path's parent.\n\nActually wait — the parent may have been created by an earlier entry in the same call (e.g., entry 1 is `a.txt` in dir `d/`, entry 2 is `d/sub/b.txt` where `d/sub` is newly created). Parent cleanup in `rollback` against `prior` entries handles those. The failed entry's parent was also created by this call (via `create_dir_all`), so it should be included in cleanup.\n\nSo in the write-fail branch, add the failed path (as `Prior::Created`, but without doing the delete… just for cleanup purposes) to the cleanup path list. The simplest thing: make `rollback` take an optional extra \"cleanup-only\" path, or always include the failed path's parent in the cleanup.\n\nActually, simpler design: on write failure, the tree state is: files 1..n-1 are written with new content, file n is unchanged. Rollback: restore files 1..n-1 (write back / delete), then clean up empty directories for all touched paths including the failed path. The \"restored\"/\"failed\" lists in `RollbackIncomplete` are about file restoration, so only actually-written files belong there.\n\nRestructure: separate \"restore\" (restore the written files) from \"cleanup\" (delete empty directories for all paths including the failed one).\n\n2. **`Prior::Created` delete when file does not exist**: since `create_dir_all` succeeded and the file did not exist, we only reach the write-fail branch if the file is not in the rollback list. And on write success we push `(path, prior_state)`. Rollback only restores previously-written files. If a previously-written file disappears from under us… edge case, ignore.\n\nBut wait, there is actually a subtle issue: `Prior::Created` means we wrote the file (success), so the file exists and the delete works.\n\n3. **Read failure of an existing file** (line 105): the file exists but is not readable → we cannot take a backup. Per spec, restore exact prior bytes; we can't, so the write must not proceed → roll back what we did and return `WriteFailed` (with a message about read failure). This is the honest approach.\n\n4. **`path.exists()` for a directory**: if a target path is an existing directory, `fs::read` fails (EISDIR) → treated as \"cannot back up\" → WriteFailed. Hmm, but should writing to a directory path be a \"write failed\" error? `fs::write` to a directory would fail anyway (EISDIR). Either way the behavior is correct.\n\n5. **cleanup_parents on success path**: after successful writes, directories that were created remain (that's fine — they're part of the intended tree, parent dirs of the new files are expected). No cleanup needed on success.\n\n6. **Empty input**: `Ok(())`. ✓\n\n7. **First entry fails** (visible test): parent `block` is a file → `create_dir_all` fails → `prior` is empty → rollback returns (empty, empty) → `WriteFailed`. ✓ And no stray files. ✓\n\nNow let me fix the write-fail branch: the failed path must not appear in `restored`/`failed` (it was not written), but its parent must be cleaned up.\n\nRedesign `rollback` to take `restore: &[(PathBuf, &Prior)]` and `all_touched: &[PathBuf]` for cleanup:\n\nActually simpler: keep `rollback(prior: &[(PathBuf, &Prior)])` doing restores + parent cleanup for those paths. In the write-fail branch, call `rollback(&list_of_written_files)` and additionally do a best-effort `remove_dir` on the failed path's parent.\n\nLet me also double-check the **`Restored`/`failed` lists in `RollbackIncomplete`**: the spec says \"`restored` lists what was restored, `failed` lists what could not be.\" So these are file paths. The write-failure branch includes only actually-written files. ✓\n\nAnother subtlety: in `rollback`, cleanup uses all paths including failed ones — good, because even failed-restore entries might have new parent directories.\n\nLet me also consider: in the write-fail branch, should the failed file's own parent directory be cleaned? Yes. Add:\n\n```rust\n// Clean up the failed path's parent dir too (may have been created by this call).\nif let Some(parent) = path.parent().filter(|p| !p.as_os_str().is_empty()) {\n    let _ = std::fs::remove_dir(parent);\n}\n```\n\nOnly if the dir is empty — `remove_dir` fails otherwise. Good.\n\nAlso the create_dir_all failure branch: prior may be non-empty (earlier entries written, and now the parent of entry n cannot be created). Rollback restores entries 1..n-1 and cleans up their parents. The failed entry's parent: `create_dir_all` may have partially created some directories before failing! Example: `d/x/y/z/file` where `d/x` exists, `d/x/y` gets created, and `create_dir_all` fails at `z` (because somewhere under `d/x` there's a file named `y`). In that case, `d/x/y` is a stray empty directory created by this call. Best-effort `remove_dir` on `path.parent()` handles the immediate parent, but deeper partial directories remain. To be thorough, clean up ancestors of the parent from deepest: iterate ancestors of the parent directory, best-effort `remove_dir` each. `remove_dir` fails on non-empty, so it's safe.\n\nOK let me rewrite lib.rs with these fixes.\n\n","time":{"start":1789805339021,"end":1789805372175}}}
